```python
import json

def get_solar_system_simulation():
    """
    Generates a high-performance solar system simulation using layered canvases.
    Includes a technical guide on libraries (Three.js, D3.js), performance 
    optimization, and rendering challenges.
    """

    planets = [
        {"name": "Mercury", "distance": 0.39, "period": 88, "radius": 0.38, "color": "#A5A5A5"},
        {"name": "Venus", "distance": 0.72, "period": 224.7, "radius": 0.95, "color": "#E3BB76"},
        {"name": "Earth", "distance": 1.00, "period": 365.2, "radius": 1.00, "color": "#2271B3"},
        {"name": "Mars", "distance": 1.52, "period": 687, "radius": 0.53, "color": "#E27B58"},
        {"name": "Jupiter", "distance": 5.20, "period": 4331, "radius": 11.21, "color": "#D39C7E"},
        {"name": "Saturn", "distance": 9.54, "period": 10747, "radius": 9.45, "color": "#C5AB6E"},
        {"name": "Uranus", "distance": 19.22, "period": 30589, "radius": 4.01, "color": "#BBE1E4"},
        {"name": "Neptune", "distance": 30.06, "period": 59800, "radius": 3.88, "color": "#6081FF"},
    ]

    html_content = f"""
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Solar System: Technical Blueprint</title>
    <style>
        body {{ margin: 0; background: #020205; color: #eee; overflow: hidden; font-family: 'Segoe UI', sans-serif; }}

        /* Layered Canvas Setup */
        .canvas-container {{ position: relative; width: 100vw; height: 100vh; }}
        canvas {{ position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; display: block; }}

        #bgCanvas {{ z-index: 1; }} /* Static elements: Stars, Orbits, Sun */
        #fgCanvas {{ z-index: 2; }} /* Dynamic elements: Moving Planets */

        .ui-overlay {{ 
            position: absolute; top: 20px; left: 20px; 
            background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.92); padding: 20px; 
            border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #444;
            width: 420px; max-height: 90vh; overflow-y: auto;
            box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(0,0,0,0.8);
            z-index: 10;
        }}

        h1 {{ margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 18px; color: #FFD700; border-bottom: 1px solid #333; padding-bottom: 5px; }}
        h2 {{ font-size: 13px; color: #4db8ff; margin-top: 18px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1px; }}
        p, li {{ font-size: 11.5px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 8px 0; color: #bbb; }}
        .highlight {{ color: #fff; font-weight: bold; }}
        code {{ background: #1a1a1a; color: #50fa7b; padding: 2px 4px; border-radius: 4px; font-family: monospace; }}

        ::-webkit-scrollbar {{ width: 6px; }}
        ::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {{ background: #444; border-radius: 3px; }}
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="ui-overlay">
        <h1>Technical Blueprint</h1>

        <h2>1. Enhancing with Libraries</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><span class="highlight">Three.js (3D):</span> Best for realistic rendering. Use <code>SphereGeometry</code> and <code>PointLight</code> to create 3D planets with day/night cycles and atmospheric scattering (Rayleigh scattering).</li>
            <li><span class="highlight">D3.js (Data):</span> Ideal for handling <b>Logarithmic Scales</b>. Use <code>d3.scaleLog()</code> to map AU distances to pixels while maintaining interactive zoom/pan capabilities.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>2. Performance Optimization</h2>
        <p><span class="highlight">Layered Canvases:</span> This implementation uses two canvases. By drawing orbits and stars only once on the <code>bgCanvas</code>, we reduce the per-frame GPU load significantly.</p>
        <p><span class="highlight">Delta Time (dt):</span> Logic is tied to real-world time rather than frame rate, ensuring consistent speed across 60Hz and 144Hz monitors.</p>

        <h2>3. Challenges: Scale & Data</h2>
        <p><span class="highlight">Realistic Rendering:</span> Space is vast. Linear scales make planets invisible. We use <b>Non-linear scaling</b> (Log10) for distances to keep the system visible within the viewport.</p>
        <p><span class="highlight">Large Datasets:</span> Integrating JPL Horizons API results in large payloads. Use <b>Web Workers</b> to parse ASCII ephemeris data in a background thread to prevent UI stuttering.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="canvas-container">
        <canvas id="bgCanvas"></canvas>
        <canvas id="fgCanvas"></canvas>
    </div>

    <script>
        const bgCanvas = document.getElementById('bgCanvas');
        const fgCanvas = document.getElementById('fgCanvas');
        const bgCtx = bgCanvas.getContext('2d');
        const fgCtx = fgCanvas.getContext('2d');
        const planetsData = {json.dumps(planets)};

        let lastTime = 0;
        let simulationTime = 0;
        const speedMultiplier = 45; 

        function resize() {{
            const dpr = window.devicePixelRatio || 1;
            [bgCanvas, fgCanvas].forEach(c => {{
                c.width = window.innerWidth * dpr;
                c.height = window.innerHeight * dpr;
                c.style.width = window.innerWidth + 'px';
                c.style.height = window.innerHeight + 'px';
            }});
            bgCtx.scale(dpr, dpr);
            fgCtx.scale(dpr, dpr);
            drawStaticBackground();
        }}

        function drawStaticBackground() {{
            const w = window.innerWidth;
            const h = window.innerHeight;
            const centerX = w / 2;
            const centerY = h / 2;

            // Clear Background
            bgCtx.fillStyle = "#020205";
            bgCtx.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);

            // Draw Starfield
            bgCtx.fillStyle = "white";
            for(let i=0; i<400; i++) {{
                bgCtx.beginPath();
                bgCtx.arc(Math.random() * w, Math.random() * h, Math.random() * 1.2, 0, Math.PI * 2);
                bgCtx.fill();
            }}

            // Draw Orbits
            planetsData.forEach(p => {{
                const visualDist = Math.log10(p.distance + 1) * 220 + 40;
                bgCtx.beginPath();
                bgCtx.arc(centerX, centerY, visualDist, 0, Math.PI * 2);
                bgCtx.strokeStyle = "rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08)";
                bgCtx.lineWidth = 1;
                bgCtx.stroke();
            }});

            // Draw Sun with Glow
            bgCtx.shadowBlur = 35;
            bgCtx.shadowColor = "#FFD700";
            bgCtx.fillStyle = "#FFD700";
            bgCtx.beginPath();
            bgCtx.arc(centerX, centerY, 18, 0, Math.PI * 2);
            bgCtx.fill();
            bgCtx.shadowBlur = 0;
        }}

        function animate(currentTime) {{
            const dt = (currentTime - lastTime) / 1000;
            lastTime = currentTime;
            if (!isNaN(dt)) simulationTime += dt * speedMultiplier;

            // Clear only the dynamic foreground
            fgCtx.clearRect(0, 0, window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);

            const centerX = window.innerWidth / 2;
            const centerY = window.innerHeight / 2;

            planetsData.forEach((p) => {{
                const visualDist = Math.log10(p.distance + 1) * 220 + 40;
                const angle = (simulationTime / p.period) * Math.PI * 2;
                const x = centerX + Math.cos(angle) * visualDist;
                const y = centerY + Math.sin(angle) * visualDist;

                // Planet with Radial Gradient (Simulates 3D lighting)
                const grad = fgCtx.createRadialGradient(x, y, 0, x, y, p.radius * 2);
                grad.addColorStop(0, p.color);
                grad.addColorStop(1, "black");

                fgCtx.beginPath();
                fgCtx.arc(x, y, Math.max(3, p.radius * 1.3), 0, Math.PI * 2);
                fgCtx.fillStyle = grad;
                fgCtx.fill();

                fgCtx.fillStyle = "rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5)";
                fgCtx.font = "10px Arial";
                fgCtx.fillText(p.name, x + 12, y + 4);
            }});

            requestAnimationFrame(animate);
        }}

        window.onresize = resize;
        resize();
        requestAnimationFrame(animate);
    </script>
</body>
</html>
    """
    return html_content
```

### 1. Library Suggestions
*   **Three.js (3D Rendering):** To move beyond 2D circles, Three.js allows you to use WebGL for hardware-accelerated 3D graphics. You can apply realistic textures (diffuse and specular maps) to planet spheres and use a `PointLight` at the Sun's origin to create dynamic shadows and crescent phases.
*   **D3.js (Data Visualization):** D3 is ideal for simulations requiring high interactivity. Its powerful scaling functions (`d3.scaleLog`) make it easy to toggle between "True Scale" and "Visual Scale" while handling smooth transitions and camera panning.

### 2. Performance Optimization Tips
*   **Layered Rendering:** As implemented in the code above, using two canvases—one for static orbits/stars and one for moving planets—drastically reduces the number of draw calls per frame.
*   **Delta Time ($\Delta t$):** Always calculate the time difference between frames. This prevents the simulation from running too fast on high-refresh-rate monitors (like 144Hz) or too slow on older hardware.
*   **Offscreen Buffering:** For complex visual effects like planetary rings or atmospheric glows, pre-render them to an `OffscreenCanvas` once and use `drawImage()` to stamp them onto the main canvas.

### 3. Addressing Challenges
*   **Scale and Precision:** Using a linear scale for the solar system is impossible on a standard screen. The solution is **Non-linear Scaling** (Logarithmic or Power scales). For professional simulations, maintain the real physics in the background logic and only apply scaling for the final coordinate mapping.
*   **Large Datasets (JPL Horizons):** When fetching thousands of coordinate points from the JPL Horizons API, the primary challenge is the main-thread latency. Use **Web Workers** to parse and calculate ephemerides in a background thread, ensuring the UI remains responsive at 60FPS.

To ensure a `LineRenderer` integrates perfectly with a UI Canvas, you must manage two distinct systems: the **Rendering Pipeline** (how it looks) and the **Event System** (how it interacts).

The most reliable way to layer them is using the **Screen Space - Camera** render mode. This allows World Space objects like your trajectory line to be interleaved with UI elements using **Sorting Layers** and **Z-depth offsets**.

### The Implementation

This script includes the critical `zOffset` logic to prevent clipping and uses `EventSystem` to ensure the trajectory doesn't trigger when you are interacting with UI buttons.

```python
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.EventSystems; // Required for UI interaction check

[RequireComponent(typeof(LineRenderer))]
public class TrajectoryPredictor : MonoBehaviour
{
    [Header("Physics Settings")]
    public Rigidbody2D rb;
    public float forceMultiplier = 5f;
    public int resolution = 30;
    public float step = 0.1f;

    [Header("UI & Layering")]
    public string sortingLayerName = "UI"; 
    public int orderInLayer = 10;
    [Tooltip("Moves the line closer to the camera to stay in front of UI planes")]
    public float zOffset = -1f; 

    private LineRenderer lr;
    private Vector2 startPoint;
    private Vector2 direction;
    private bool isDragging = false;

    void Awake()
    {
        lr = GetComponent<LineRenderer>();
        lr.enabled = false;

        // Configure LineRenderer for UI layering
        lr.useWorldSpace = true; 
        lr.sortingLayerName = sortingLayerName;
        lr.sortingOrder = orderInLayer;

        if (lr.material == null)
        {
            lr.material = new Material(Shader.Find("Sprites/Default"));
        }
    }

    void Update()
    {
        if (Input.GetMouseButtonDown(0))
        {
            // Interaction Check: Prevent starting a drag if clicking a UI button/element
            if (EventSystem.current != null && EventSystem.current.IsPointerOverGameObject())
            {
                return;
            }

            startPoint = Camera.main.ScreenToWorldPoint(Input.mousePosition);
            isDragging = true;
            lr.enabled = true;
        }

        if (isDragging && Input.GetMouseButton(0))
        {
            Vector2 currentMousePos = Camera.main.ScreenToWorldPoint(Input.mousePosition);
            direction = startPoint - currentMousePos;
            DrawTrajectory();
        }

        if (isDragging && Input.GetMouseButtonUp(0))
        {
            Launch();
            lr.enabled = false;
            isDragging = false;
        }
    }

    void DrawTrajectory()
    {
        lr.positionCount = resolution;
        Vector2 velocity = direction * forceMultiplier;
        Vector3[] points = new Vector3[resolution];

        for (int i = 0; i < resolution; i++)
        {
            float t = i * step;
            Vector2 pos = (Vector2)transform.position + (velocity * t) + 0.5f * Physics2D.gravity * (t * t);

            // Layering Fix: Apply zOffset to ensure the line is visible relative to the UI plane
            points[i] = new Vector3(pos.x, pos.y, transform.position.z + zOffset);
        }

        lr.SetPositions(points);
    }

    void Launch()
    {
        rb.velocity = direction * forceMultiplier;
    }
}
```

### Key Integration Steps

#### 1. Sorting Layers (The "Sandwich" Method)
To place the line exactly where you want it relative to UI elements:
1.  Go to **Project Settings > Tags and Layers**.
2.  Define layers in this order: `Background`, `Trajectory`, `Foreground`.
3.  Set your Canvas Background image to the `Background` layer.
4.  Set the script's `sortingLayerName` to `Trajectory`.
5.  Set your UI Buttons/HUD elements to the `Foreground` layer.

#### 2. Canvas Setup
*   **Render Mode:** Change to **Screen Space - Camera**.
*   **Render Camera:** Assign your **Main Camera**.
*   **Plane Distance:** Set this to a value (e.g., 10) that places the UI at the same depth as your game objects. The `zOffset` in the script will then move the line slightly forward from that plane to ensure it doesn't flicker or clip through the UI.

#### 3. Handling UI Interaction
The `EventSystem.current.IsPointerOverGameObject()` check is essential. It detects if the mouse is currently over a UI element that has "Raycast Target" enabled. This prevents the "launch" logic from triggering when the player is simply trying to click a menu button or inventory slot.

#### 4. Visual Dotted Effect
To get the dotted look within this UI setup:
1.  Create a **Material** using the `UI/Default` shader.
2.  Assign a circle sprite to the texture slot.
3.  On the **Line Renderer**, set **Texture Mode** to **Tile**.
4.  Apply this material to the Line Renderer component. This ensures the dots scale correctly even when the UI camera zooms or moves.
